WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.409
"Sir Francis," said Mr. Fogg quietly, "we will look around for some transport to Allahabad."

2
00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.297
"Mr. Fogg, this delay is to your disadvantage!" said Sir Francis.

3
00:00:12.298 --> 00:00:16.305
"No, Sir Francis, it was foreseen," replied Fogg.

4
00:00:16.306 --> 00:00:21.995
"What!" cried Sir Francis. "You knew that the railway—"

5
00:00:21.996 --> 00:00:28.944
"Not at all," said Mr. Fogg. "But I knew that some obstacle would come up eventually.

6
00:00:28.945 --> 00:00:34.635
Nothing is lost. I have two days, which I have gained, to sacrifice.

7
00:00:34.636 --> 00:00:39.238
A steamer leaves Calcutta for Hong Kong at noon on the twenty-fifth.

8
00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:44.151
This is the twenty-second so we shall reach Calcutta in time."

9
00:00:44.152 --> 00:00:49.058
There was nothing Sir Francis could say to such a confident response.

10
00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:53.614
The three men went separate ways to search the village for a vehicle.

11
00:00:53.615 --> 00:00:56.873
Unfortunately, while they had been talking with the conductor,

12
00:00:56.874 --> 00:01:03.156
the other passengers had already hired all the available wagons, carriages, and ponies.

13
00:01:03.157 --> 00:01:07.894
Sir Francis and Mr. Fogg returned to the train with no luck. Passepartout,

14
00:01:07.895 --> 00:01:12.450
however, returned from his search with a suggestion. "Monsieur,

15
00:01:12.451 --> 00:01:17.571
I think I have found transport for us," he said. "What

16
00:01:17.572 --> 00:01:20.249
would that be?" asked Fogg. "An

17
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:26.416
elephant!" said Passepartout. "It belongs to an Indian man who lives close by." "Let's

18
00:01:26.417 --> 00:01:31.045
go and see the elephant," replied Mr. Fogg. Passepartout

19
00:01:31.046 --> 00:01:38.208
led them to a small hut, near which a tall elephant stood behind a high gate. The

20
00:01:38.209 --> 00:01:47.174
elephant's owner said its name was Kiouni, Mr. Fogg resolved immediately to hire him and offered ten pounds an hour. The

21
00:01:47.175 --> 00:01:53.760
owner, however, refused and kept refusing every time Mr. Fogg raised his offer. When

22
00:01:53.761 --> 00:01:58.693
Fogg offered forty pounds an hour, the owner still wouldn't agree. Mr.

23
00:01:58.694 --> 00:02:04.945
Fogg, without getting the least bit upset, proposed to purchase the elephant completely. He

24
00:02:04.946 --> 00:02:09.456
offered one thousand pounds but was refused again. Passepartout

25
00:02:09.457 --> 00:02:14.785
listened in amazement as his master continued to raise the price. Finally

26
00:02:14.786 --> 00:02:19.912
the man agreed to sell the elephant for two thousand pounds. Mr.

27
00:02:19.913 --> 00:02:23.786
Fogg paid with bank notes from the carpetbag. Kiouni

28
00:02:23.787 --> 00:02:27.193
was led out and equipped for the journey. After

29
00:02:27.194 --> 00:02:33.921
hiring a guide and purchasing a few provisions, they set off at a quick pace. Instead

30
00:02:33.922 --> 00:02:42.785
of following the wide, curving path that workers had cleared for the railway, their guide led them directly through the jungle. Growls

31
00:02:42.786 --> 00:02:48.539
from panthers and the chatter of monkeys occasionally broke the jungle's stillness. After

32
00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.636
a day of travelling, they stopped for the night. The

33
00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:54.902
guide lit a fire, and they prepared a supper. They

34
00:02:54.903 --> 00:02:59.001
ate hungrily and were snoring loudly soon after. The

35
00:02:59.002 --> 00:03:02.990
journey started again at six o'clock the next morning. Kiouni

36
00:03:02.991 --> 00:03:09.674
resumed his quick pace, and their guide said they might reach Allahabad that evening. Fogg

37
00:03:09.675 --> 00:03:14.157
and his companions continued without any unpleasant encounters. The

38
00:03:14.158 --> 00:03:21.453
journey seemed on the point of success when the elephant became restless and stopped. It was four o'clock. "What's the

39
00:03:21.454 --> 00:03:25.002
matter?" asked Sir Francis. "I'm not

40
00:03:25.003 --> 00:03:29.344
sure, sir," replied the guide, listening carefully. A sound

41
00:03:29.345 --> 00:03:32.550
was coming through the thick bushes. The guide

42
00:03:32.551 --> 00:03:38.708
jumped down, tied the elephant to a tree, and disappeared into the dense brush. Mr. Fogg

43
00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:48.402
waited patiently without a word. The sound grew closer—it was a rhythmic concert of human voices and musical instruments. The guide

44
00:03:48.403 --> 00:03:56.349
returned in a rush. "A procession of Brahmins is coming this way," he said. "We must hide!" Through the

45
00:03:56.350 --> 00:04:00.875
branches of thick foliage, they watched the procession pass. Behind a

46
00:04:00.876 --> 00:04:07.867
tall leader danced a group of bare-chested priests, their bodies striped with dark red mud or paint. A young

47
00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:12.857
woman followed, led by four men in sumptuous Oriental clothes. Each was

48
00:04:12.858 --> 00:04:16.139
armed with a curved sword at his waist. The woman's

49
00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:22.984
neck, ears, arms, hands, and toes were loaded with jewels, bracelets, and rings. She seemed

50
00:04:22.985 --> 00:04:28.619
weak, as if only half-awake, and two guards held her arms. Musicians playing

51
00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:34.177
tambourines and cymbals followed at the end of the procession. After the

52
00:04:34.178 --> 00:04:40.917
Brahmins had passed, Sir Francis said, "It is a suttee , a voluntary human sacrifice. The

53
00:04:40.918 --> 00:04:45.560
woman we have just seen will be burned alive tomorrow at dawn. Her

54
00:04:45.561 --> 00:04:50.505
husband has died, and she is to pass into the afterlife with him." "That

55
00:04:50.506 --> 00:04:59.890
is true," said the guide. "But not all suttees are voluntary. Everyone in Kholby has heard about this suttee. This

56
00:04:59.891 --> 00:05:06.647
young widow is not willing. She has been drugged so that she won’t resist her fate." The

57
00:05:06.648 --> 00:05:12.419
guide climbed onto the neck of Kiouni and prepared to continue their journey. Mr.

58
00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:16.601
Fogg said, "Suppose we save the woman." "Save

59
00:05:16.602 --> 00:05:20.647
the woman, Mr. Fogg?" said Sir Francis. "I

60
00:05:20.648 --> 00:05:25.962
have 12 extra hours, which I can devote to saving this woman," said Fogg. "You

61
00:05:25.963 --> 00:05:29.988
are a man of heart!" said Sir Francis. "Sometimes," replied

62
00:05:29.989 --> 00:05:39.638
Mr. Fogg quietly. "When I have the time." Passepartout's Guidebook

63
00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:46.847
What a day that was! Riding an elephant through the jungle and finding a young woman about to be burned alive!

64
00:05:46.848 --> 00:05:54.254
Although the majority of Indian widows are not burned, an Indian woman can never marry again if her husband dies.

65
00:05:54.255 --> 00:05:58.978
According to Indian tradition, she has to shave her head and wear a white sari.

66
00:05:58.979 --> 00:06:02.415
And it is quite common for young girls to be married to older men.

67
00:06:02.416 --> 00:06:08.765
So if a young girl’s husband dies, she will wind up having to spend most of her life all alone.

68
00:06:08.766 --> 00:06:11.981
Widows are also seen as a bad omen.

69
00:06:11.982 --> 00:06:19.816
They are usually banished from their families, and many of them go to the city of Vrindavan, where they can work in the city's numerous temples.

70
00:06:19.817 --> 00:06:27.688
Because of this, Vrindavan is sometimes called "the city of widows."

